        <span class="service-index">01 / Implementation</span>
        <div class="card-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-cubes" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
        <h3>HubSpot implementation and onboarding</h3>
        <p class="service__for"><strong>For:</strong> New portals, or a portal that was set up quickly and never properly architected.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>What it involves</dt><dd>We design and build the full structure: objects and custom properties, deal pipelines and stages, lifecycle stages and lead statuses, teams and permissions, forms and lists, workflows and sequences, then the dashboards on top.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What changes</dt><dd>Your reps work in a CRM that matches how they actually sell, so they use it. Your marketing team can segment on data that is reliably there. Your leadership gets reporting that does not need a spreadsheet check first.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Typically includes</dt><dd>Portal audit, data model design, pipeline and lifecycle architecture, permission structure, core automation, dashboard build, SOPs, and team training.</dd></div>
        </dl>
                <a class="link-arrow" href="/solutions/hubspot-implementation/" style="margin-top:20px">HubSpot implementation services <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
        </article>
      <article class="card service" id="migration">
        <span class="service-index">02 / Migration</span>
        <div class="card-icon card-icon--blue"><i class="fa-solid fa-right-left" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
        <h3>CRM migration</h3>
        <p class="service__for"><strong>For:</strong> Moving to HubSpot from Salesforce, Pipedrive, Zoho, a legacy CRM, or spreadsheets.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>What it involves</dt><dd>We audit the source system, map every field to its HubSpot destination, resolve duplicates before import rather than after, rebuild automation and reporting in the new environment, and test the whole thing in a sandbox before cutover.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What changes</dt><dd>Your team lands in a CRM that works on day one, with the history that matters intact and the automation already running. Migrations fail when data arrives without structure and everyone quietly goes back to the old system. That is the failure we are engineered to prevent.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Typically includes</dt><dd>Source audit, field mapping sheet, deduplication and enrichment, sandbox import and QA, automation and reporting rebuild, cutover plan, and training.</dd></div>
        </dl>
                <a class="link-arrow" href="/solutions/hubspot-migration/" style="margin-top:20px">HubSpot migration services <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
        </article>
      <article class="card service" id="cleanup">
        <span class="service-index">03 / Data quality</span>
        <div class="card-icon card-icon--cyan"><i class="fa-solid fa-broom" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
        <h3>CRM cleanup and data quality</h3>
        <p class="service__for"><strong>For:</strong> Portals where reporting cannot be trusted, duplicates are widespread, or fields were never governed.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>What it involves</dt><dd>We audit record quality across contacts, companies, and deals, deduplicate systematically rather than by hand, standardize field values, then install the validation rules, required properties, and naming conventions that keep it clean.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What changes</dt><dd>Reporting becomes something you act on rather than something you verify. Automation stops misfiring on bad records. Reps stop finding three versions of the same account.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Typically includes</dt><dd>Data quality audit, deduplication, field standardization, validation and governance rules, ongoing data hygiene workflows, and a governance SOP.</dd></div>
        </dl>
                <a class="link-arrow" href="/solutions/hubspot-crm-cleanup/" style="margin-top:20px">HubSpot data cleanup <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
        </article>
      <article class="card service" id="revops">
        <span class="service-index">04 / RevOps</span>
        <div class="card-icon card-icon--green"><i class="fa-solid fa-chart-line" aria-hidden="true"></i></div>
        <h3>RevOps infrastructure, lead to close</h3>
        <p class="service__for"><strong>For:</strong> Companies where marketing, sales, and service each work well alone but do not connect into one picture.</p>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>What it involves</dt><dd>We build the connective layer across the full revenue path: lifecycle stage definitions everyone agrees on, lead scoring and routing with SLAs and fallback owners, sales and marketing automation, attribution reporting, and the executive dashboards on top.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>What changes</dt><dd>Qualified leads reach the right rep in minutes instead of sitting unassigned. Leadership can see conversion at every stage and where deals stall. Marketing can tie spend to closed revenue rather than to form fills.</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Typically includes</dt><dd>Lifecycle architecture, lead scoring and routing, SLA and escalation design, sales and marketing automation, attribution setup, executive dashboards, and documentation.</dd></div>
        </dl>
                <a class="link-arrow" href="/solutions/revops/" style="margin-top:20px">HubSpot RevOps consulting <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"></i></a>
        </article>
    </div>
